{
  "title": "Add zero-downtime release-build target for production-ish \
deployment",
  "description": "Right now the single \"production\" instance is running via \
`make dev-headless`, which is a debug build under `cargo watch`. Downsides: \
(1) debug builds are slow and memory-heavy, (2) any source change triggers a \
full restart with a visible downtime window, (3) there is no process-level \
memory cap so an OOM (e.g. the paprikarecipes export bug) silently takes the \
server down and surfaces as a 502 at the reverse proxy.\n\
\n\
We want a new make target (e.g. `make serve` or `make prod`) that:\n\
\n\
  - Builds/runs the server in release mode.\n\
  - Uses socket activation so restarts are ~zero-downtime: the recommended \
setup is the `listenfd` crate in the server + `systemfd --no-pid -s \
https::$PORT` in front of `cargo watch -x 'run --release'`. The kernel queues \
incoming conns while the new binary boots, existing conns drain via axum's \
graceful shutdown. Minimal code change (a small adapter around the axum \
bind).\n\
  - Sets a memory cap on the server process (systemd-run \
--property=MemoryMax=..., ulimit -v via wrapper, or similar) so an OOM \
surfaces as an immediate fast failure with a log line instead of a mystery \
502.\n\
  - Does NOT change the behavior of `make dev` / `make dev-headless`. Those \
should stay debug+cargo-watch for iteration speed.\n\
\n\
Nice-to-haves:\n\
\n\
  - Fold it into `process-compose.yaml` as an alternate profile or a separate \
compose file so the rest of the dev stack (UI, certs, seed) still composes \
cleanly.\n\
  - Add a `make serve-down` or equivalent.\n\
\n\
Context: originally surfaced while diagnosing a paprikarecipes-export OOM — \
see also the export streaming/downscaling PR that fixes the immediate memory \
blow-up. Memory cap here is defense in depth so future regressions fail \
loudly.",
  "status": "open",
